        "client_msg_id": "0034ea1f-1c97-4f47-b688-641de2a3a8ad",
        "text": "As I said to Geoffrey on X about this article: even before LLMs, agents, copilots, buddies, I've personally always been an \"Extend or Augment Reality\"-philosophy person. Given the challenge to our community that LLMs bring, this will hopefully be an interesting thread, as this juxtaposition is quite polarising I think?",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1753674715.726079",
        "parent_user_id": "U018S42NMMM",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "bTJIG",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "As I said to Geoffrey on X about this article: even before LLMs, agents, copilots, buddies, I've personally always been an \"Extend or Augment Reality\"-philosophy person. Given the challenge to our community that LLMs bring, this will hopefully be an interesting thread, as this juxtaposition is quite polarising I think?"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1753728066.543149",
        "edited": {
            "user": "UE6EFEPTQ",
            "ts": "1753728157.000000"
        },
        "client_msg_id": "2b467eb4-8dfe-4f60-aeac-18595c723592",
        "text": "In the sense that one can see imperative programming languages as very much philosophically-aligned to agents: you \"tell\" the computer what to do and it slavishly follows instructions, as your programmable agent on-the-metal. The dual or inverse, declarative programming, is much more holistic and humane IMO, as you're dealing with live domain \"stuff\" directly - it augments your reality. AI agents are an extreme of imperative in the same way HUDs or AR are an extreme of declarative. Hence more extreme polarisation.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1753674715.726079",
        "parent_user_id": "U018S42NMMM",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "0xIPQ",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "In the sense that one can see imperative programming languages as very much philosophically-aligned to agents: you \"tell\" the computer what to do and it slavishly follows instructions, as your programmable agent on-the-metal. The dual or inverse, declarative programming, is much more holistic and humane IMO, as you're dealing with live domain \"stuff\" directly - it augments your reality. AI agents are an extreme of imperative in the same way HUDs or AR are an extreme of declarative. Hence more extreme polarisation."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
